# Snyk policy file — SCA license finding ignores
# https://docs.snyk.io/snyk-cli/commands/policy
version: v1.25.0
ignore:
  'snyk:lic:npm:rasha:MPL-2.0':
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency of @okta/okta-sdk-nodejs. MPL-2.0 is weak
          copyleft compatible with project use. Package is unmodified.
  'snyk:lic:npm:eckles:MPL-2.0':
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency of @okta/okta-sdk-nodejs. MPL-2.0 is weak
          copyleft compatible with project use. Package is unmodified.
  SNYK-JS-JSONBIGINT-6148131:
    - '*':
        reason: 'False positive - json-bigint not in package-lock.json or dependency tree'
        expires: 2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-XML2JS-5414874:
    - '*':
        reason: 'False positive - xml2js not in package-lock.json or dependency tree'
        expires: 2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-UUID-16133035:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency via launchdarkly-js-sdk-common, @azure/msal-node,
          and njwt. No fixed version available from upstream packages.
        expires: 2026-05-23T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-JSYAML-18313070:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency via @okta/okta-sdk-nodejs@8.1.0 and
          cosmiconfig@8.3.6 (via vite-plugin-svgr), both pinned to js-yaml
          ^4.1.0. Fix requires js-yaml 5.2.2, a major version bump with
          breaking API/schema changes (see js-yaml migrate_v4_to_v5 guide)
          that needs compatibility validation before adoption. 5.2.2 was
          also published 2026-07-23, inside CAMS's 14/30-day minimum
          release age window. js-yaml only parses local, non-attacker-
          controlled config files here (okta config-loader.js, cosmiconfig
          build tooling), so exploitability is low in the interim.
        expires: 2026-08-24T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-NODEFORGE-19635204:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency via @okta/okta-sdk-nodejs@8.2.0, which
          hard-depends on node-jose for its own package.json (every
          node-jose version, 0.9.0 through latest, requires node-forge,
          so no version swap avoids this). CVE-2026-85393 is an RSA
          signature-verification bypass, but our only reachable node-jose
          call is JWS.createSign for outbound DPoP proof signing
          (@okta/okta-sdk-nodejs/src/dpop.js) — JWS.createVerify is never
          invoked anywhere in our usage. Re-verified 2026-10-14: still no
          fixed node-forge version on npm (latest remains 1.4.0). Upstream
          fix PR digitalbazaar/forge#1152 is still open/unmerged against
          digitalbazaar/forge#1149, despite having multiple reviewer
          approvals. Keeping the 2-week renewal window given the PR looks
          close to merging.
        expires: 2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-SPRINTFJS-20532016:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Improper Validation of Specified Quantity in Input in sprintf-js
          (CVE-2026-97058): an attacker-controlled precision specifier in
          the format string (e.g. '%.999999f') is passed unvalidated to
          toFixed/toExponential/toPrecision, triggering an uncaught
          RangeError (DoS). No fixed version exists upstream; tracked by
          sprintf.js#237 (opened 2026-09-16, still open, low activity).
          Not reachable in CAMS: sprintf-js is not a direct dependency —
          its only consumer in the lockfile is mssql@12.7.2 >
          tedious@20.0.0. tedious's two call sites
          (lib/metadata-parser.js) both pass hardcoded literal format
          strings with no precision specifier ('Unrecognised data type
          0x%02X', 'Unrecognised type %s'); only the substituted value
          (a byte/name parsed off the TDS wire) varies, never the format
          string itself. The vulnerable code path — attacker control of
          the precision specifier — is unreachable through this usage.
          Re-check in 30 days for upstream progress on #237 or a fixed
          release.
        expires: 2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-REACTROUTER-18313151:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Transitive dependency via user-interface's react-router-dom,
          now at 7.18.4 (hoisted into test/e2e and test/uswds), which
          still pins react-router below the 8.3.0 fix — react-router-dom
          has never published an 8.x release; v8 unifies routing into
          the react-router package directly. Migration off
          react-router-dom to react-router v8 is tracked by CAMS-854 and
          in progress (only a small fraction of imports converted so
          far). Re-verified 2026-09-30: this ignore lapsed 2026-09-10
          without renewal; renewing now. The CVE is an RSC/server-actions
          CSRF bypass; CAMS is a client-only SPA that uses neither RSC
          nor server actions, so exploitability remains low in the
          interim.
        expires: 2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-QS-20943482:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Uncaught Exception in qs@6.16.0, bundled by express@5.2.1 and
          body-parser@2.3.0 (node_modules/express/node_modules/qs,
          node_modules/body-parser/node_modules/qs). The fix (qs 6.16.1
          changelog: "stringify: arrayFormat: 'comma': do not throw on
          elements with no callable toString") is scoped to
          qs.stringify() called with arrayFormat: 'comma'. Not
          reachable here: express and body-parser only call qs.parse()
          to read incoming query strings/bodies, never qs.stringify().
          No CAMS source calls qs directly or passes arrayFormat
          anywhere (verified by grep across backend, common,
          user-interface, dev-tools, dora-metrics, test). The only other
          qs consumer in the lockfile is typed-rest-client (pulled in
          transitively by @stryker-mutator/core, dev-only), which
          defaults arrayFormat to 'repeat', not 'comma', and is never
          invoked by CAMS code. Fixed version 6.16.1 was published
          2026-10-14 and is blocked by CAMS's 14-day minimum npm
          release-age policy (.npmrc min-release-age=14) until
          2026-10-28. Bump the root package.json qs override from
          6.16.0 to 6.16.1 and remove this ignore once the embargo
          lifts.
        expires: 2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-BODYPARSER-21431495:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Uncaught Exception (DoS) in body-parser@2.3.0, pinned by the
          root package.json override and pulled in by express@5.2.1. A
          compressed request body with trailing bytes makes the
          decompression stream emit an unhandled error that crashes the
          Node process. Not reachable in any deployed environment: the
          deployed API runs on Azure Functions, and nothing under
          backend/function-apps or backend/lib imports express. The only
          express servers are backend/express/server.ts (local dev via
          npm run start:express, and the BDD harness in
          test/bdd/helpers/api-server.ts) and the test/ui-sandbox
          fake-okta server, all local or test only. Fixed version 2.4.0
          was published 2026-10-22 and is blocked by CAMS's 14-day
          minimum npm release-age policy (.npmrc min-release-age=14)
          until 2026-11-05. It requires qs ^6.16.1, so bump the root
          package.json overrides body-parser 2.3.0 -> 2.4.0 and qs
          6.16.0 -> 6.16.1 together, then remove this ignore and the
          SNYK-JS-QS-20943482 ignore.
        expires: 2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-QS-21755395:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output (CVE-2026-110910) in
          qs@6.16.0, pinned by the root package.json override and pulled
          in by express@5.3.0 and body-parser@2.3.0. The flaw is in
          qs.stringify() when called with { allowEmptyArrays: true }: an
          attacker-controlled object key containing '&', '=', or '#' is
          not encoded, letting the key name inject extra query-string
          parameters or a URL fragment into the output. Not reachable in
          CAMS: express and body-parser only call qs.parse() to read
          incoming query strings/bodies, never qs.stringify() (verified
          by grep across backend, common, user-interface, dev-tools,
          dora-metrics, test — no CAMS source calls qs.stringify or
          passes allowEmptyArrays anywhere). The only other qs consumer
          in the lockfile is typed-rest-client (pulled in transitively by
          @stryker-mutator/core, dev-only), which never calls stringify
          with allowEmptyArrays either. Fixed version 6.17.0 was
          published 2026-10-27 and is blocked by CAMS's 14-day minimum
          npm release-age policy (.npmrc min-release-age=14) until
          2026-11-10. Bump the root package.json qs override from
          6.16.0 to 6.17.0 (or 6.16.1, once that also clears the embargo
          — see SNYK-JS-QS-20943482) and remove this ignore once the
          embargo lifts.
        expires: 2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z
  SNYK-JS-AZUREMSALNODE-21689163:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          Origin Validation Error (CVE-2026-109461) in
          @azure/msal-node@6.0.0, pulled in transitively by
          @azure/identity@4.13.3 (pinned to msal-node ^6.0.0). Flagged
          across backend (node), dev-tools, and test/e2e (via mssql >
          tedious > @azure/identity). No fixed version available from
          upstream: @azure/identity's latest release (4.14.0) still pins
          msal-node ^6.0.0, and the fix (7.1.0) is a major version bump
          npm's ^6.0.0 range won't resolve to; no @azure/identity release
          depends on msal-node >=7.1.0 yet. The flaw is in
          ConfidentialClientApplication.acquireTokenByCode, which fails
          to sanitize the caller-supplied authCodePayload during an
          authorization-code token exchange. Not reachable in CAMS: every
          @azure/identity usage — the explicit DefaultAzureCredential in
          backend/lib/adapters/gateways/monitor/acs-bounce-query.gateway.ts,
          and the azure-active-directory-default auth type used by
          mssql/tedious across backend, dev-tools, and test/e2e (see
          application-configuration.ts, sql-config.ts,
          seed-sqlserver.ts) — resolves to DefaultAzureCredential's
          client-credential/managed-identity token acquisition, never
          the authorization-code flow. Re-check for an @azure/identity
          release that bumps msal-node to >=7.1.0.
        expires: 2026-11-23T00:00:00.000Z
